li><\\/ul><p>\\u00a0<\\/p><ul><li>\\u00a0<ul><li>\\u00a0<\\/li><\\/ul><\\/li><li>\\u00a0<\\/li><li>\\u00a0<\\/li><li>\\u00a0<\\/li><\\/ul><\\/td><\\/tr><tr><td><p><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Portal:Hinduism\\\"><strong>Other topics<\\/strong><\\/a><\\/p><p>\\u00a0<\\/p><ul><li>\\u00a0<\\/li><li>\\u00a0<\\/li><li>\\u00a0<\\/li><\\/ul><p>\\u00a0<\\/p><ul><li>\\u00a0<\\/li><\\/ul><\\/td><\\/tr><tr><td><ul><li><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Glossary_of_Hinduism_terms\\\"><strong>Glossary<\\/strong><\\/a><\\/li><li><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Outline_of_Hinduism\\\"><strong>Outline<\\/strong><\\/a><\\/li><li><strong>\\u00a0<\\/strong><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Portal:Hinduism\\\"><strong>Hinduism portal<\\/strong><\\/a><\\/li><\\/ul><\\/td><\\/tr><tr><td><ul><li><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Template:Hinduism\\\">v<\\/a><\\/li><li><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Template_talk:Hinduism\\\">t<\\/a><\\/li><li><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Special:EditPage\\/Template:Hinduism\\\">e<\\/a><\\/li><\\/ul><\\/td><\\/tr><\\/tbody><\\/table><\\/figure><figure class=\\\"table\\\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Deepavali<\\/strong><\\/th><th>\\u00a0<\\/th><\\/tr><\\/thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/UNESCO_Intangible_Cultural_Heritage_Lists\\\"><strong>UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage<\\/strong><\\/a><\\/td><td>\\u00a0<\\/td><\\/tr><tr><td>Country<\\/td><td><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/India\\\">India<\\/a><\\/td><\\/tr><tr><td>Reference<\\/td><td><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/ich.unesco.org\\/en\\/RL\\/02312\\\">02312<\\/a><\\/td><\\/tr><tr><td>Region<\\/td><td><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Template:UNESCO_Representative_List_of_the_Intangible_Cultural_Heritage_of_Humanity\\/APA\\\">Asia and the Pacific<\\/a><\\/td><\\/tr><tr><td>Inscription history<\\/td><td>\\u00a0<\\/td><\\/tr><tr><td>Inscription<\\/td><td>2025 (20th session)<\\/td><\\/tr><tr><td><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/UNESCO_Intangible_Cultural_Heritage_Lists\\\">List<\\/a><\\/td><td>Representative<\\/td><\\/tr><\\/tbody><\\/table><\\/figure><p><strong>Dipavali<\\/strong> (<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/IAST\\\">IAST<\\/a>: <i>D\\u012bp\\u0101val\\u012b<\\/i>),<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Diwali#cite_note-4\\\">[a]<\\/a> commonly known as <strong>Diwali<\\/strong> (<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Help:IPA\\/English\\\">\\/d\\u026a\\u02c8w\\u0251\\u02d0li\\u02d0\\/<\\/a>),<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Diwali#cite_note-Mead-2008a-5\\\">[4]<\\/a> is the <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Hindu_festival\\\">Hindu festival<\\/a> of lights, with variations celebrated in other <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Indian_religions\\\">Indian religions<\\/a> such as <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Jainism\\\">Jainism<\\/a> and <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Sikhism\\\">Sikhism<\\/a>.<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Diwali#cite_note-6\\\">[b]<\\/a> It is associated with the spiritual victory or triumph of <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Dharma\\\"><i>Dharma<\\/i><\\/a> over <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Adharma\\\"><i>Adharma<\\/i><\\/a>, light over darkness, good over evil, and knowledge over ignorance.<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Diwali#cite_note-Heiligman31-7\\\">[5]<\\/a><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Diwali#cite_note-htoday-8\\\">[6]<\\/a><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Diwali#cite_note-Mead-2008b-9\\\">[7]<\\/a><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Diwali#cite_note-melton252-10\\\">[8]<\\/a> Diwali is celebrated during the <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Hindu_calendar\\\">Hindu lunisolar<\\/a> months of <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Ashvin_(month)\\\">Ashvin<\\/a> (according to the <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Hindu_calendar#amanta\\\">amanta<\\/a> tradition) and <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Kartika_(month)\\\">K\\u0101rtika<\\/a> \\u2013 between around mid-October and mid-November.<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Diwali#cite_note-16\\\">[14]<\\/a> The celebrations generally last five or six days.<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Diwali#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFieldhouse2017150%E2%80%93151-17\\\">[15]<\\/a><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Diwali#cite_note-18\\\">[16]<\\/a><\\/p><p>Diwali is connected to various religious events, deities, and personalities, such as being the day <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Rama\\\">Rama<\\/a> returned to his <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Kosala\\\">kingdom<\\/a> in <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Ayodhya_(Ramayana)\\\">Ayodhya<\\/a> with his wife <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Sita\\\">Sita<\\/a> and his brother <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Lakshmana\\\">Lakshmana<\\/a> after defeating the demon king <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Ravana\\\">Ravana<\\/a>.<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Diwali#cite_note-dhcd-19\\\">[17]<\\/a> It is also widely associated with <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Lakshmi\\\">Lakshmi<\\/a>, the goddess of prosperity, and <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Ganesha\\\">Ganesha<\\/a>, the god of wisdom and the remover of obstacles.<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Diwali#cite_note-Suzanne_Barchers_2013-20\\\">[18]<\\/a> Other regional traditions connect the holiday to <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Vishnu\\\">Vishnu<\\/a>, <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Krishna\\\">Krishna<\\/a>, <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Durga\\\">Durga<\\/a>, <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Shiva\\\">Shiva<\\/a>, <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Kali\\\">Kali<\\/a>, <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Hanuman\\\">Hanuman<\\/a>, <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Kubera\\\">Kubera<\\/a>, <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Yama\\\">Yama<\\/a>, <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Yami\\\">Yami<\\/a>, <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Dhanvantari\\\">Dhanvantari<\\/a>, or <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Vishvakarman\\\">Vishvakarman<\\/a>.<\\/p><p>Primarily a <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Hindus\\\">Hindu<\\/a> festival, variations of Diwali are also celebrated by adherents of other faiths.<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Diwali#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFieldhouse2017150%E2%80%93151-17\\\">[15]<\\/a> The <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Jainism\\\">Jains<\\/a> observe their <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Diwali_(Jainism)\\\">own Diwali<\\/a> which marks the final liberation of <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Mahavira\\\">Mahavira<\\/a>.<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Diwali#cite_note-Sharma2006-21\\\">[19]<\\/a><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Diwali#cite_note-N._Upadhye,_Review_1982_pp._231%E2%80%93232-22\\\">[20]<\\/a> The <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Sikhism\\\">Sikhs<\\/a> celebrate <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Bandi_Chhor_Divas\\\">Bandi Chhor Divas<\\/a> to mark the release of <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Guru_Hargobind\\\">Guru Hargobind<\\/a> from a <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Mughal_Empire\\\">Mughal<\\/a> prison.<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Diwali#cite_note-23\\\">[21]<\\/a> <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Newar_Buddhism\\\">Newar Buddhists<\\/a>, unlike other <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Buddhism\\\">Buddhists<\\/a>, celebrate Diwali by worshipping Lakshmi, while the Hindus of <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Eastern_India\\\">Eastern India<\\/a> and <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Bangladesh\\\">Bangladesh<\\/a> generally, celebrate Diwali by worshipping the <a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Kali\\\">goddess Kali<\\/a>.<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Diwali#cite_note-MK72-24\\\">[22]<\\/a><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Diwali#cite_note-diwaliBuddhist-2\\\">[2]<\\/a><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/en.wikipedia.org\\/wiki\\/Diwali#cite_note-Prem_Saran_2012_175-25\\\">[23]<\\/a><\\/p><p>During the festival, celebrants illuminate their homes,<\\/p>\"}]', 'Admin', NULL, 1, '2026-10-23 07:57:02', '2026-10-23 07:57:02', '2026-10-23 07:58:43');
